n the sunshine of popular and royal favour. The literary activity of the Jains was also remarkable in this age and they seem to have taken an active part in the education of the masses.”  “Among the Rashtrakuta emperors Amoghavarsha I was more a Jain than a Hindu. […] Many of the feudatories and officers of the Rashtrakutas were also Jains.”  “The Rashtrakuta kings were basically Hindus but very tolerant of other religions such as Jainism and Buddhism. Amoghavarsha I was attracted to Jainism, being a disciple of Jinasenacharya. Although Buddhism had declined, there were pockets of Buddhism in places like Dambal and Balligavi. Different Hindu sects such as Vaishnavism and Shaivism flourished during the period of the Rashtrakutas, and Vishnu and Shiva were popular gods. The family deity of the kings was the goddess Latana, known by different names such as Rashtrashyena and Manasa Vindyavasini. The Rashtrakutas were not averse to close relations with Arabs residing in the Sind area of northwestern India. Trade with them was encouraged, and Arab Muslims were sometimes even appointed as city administrators. Islam was slowly penetrating South India, with mosques being established in the coastal towns of Kayalpattanam and Nagore.”  “Amoghavarsha I […] belittled Buddhism, Jainism, and Hinduism, yet numerous researchers are of the assessment that actually he presumably followed Jainism.”  “In religion he [Dantidurga] shared orthodox Hindu beliefs.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1177, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1079})
