 referred to as the ‘Timbuktu of Ethiopia’ because of this status and Harar was, and still is, a major centre of Muslim pilgrimage. It was also a locus of proselytization and a nucleus of Islamisation from an unconfirmed date, perhaps from the twelfth century and the migration of the Muslim Argobba into the region, or perhaps later in the fifteenth to sixteenth century, a more likely date for its foundation based on the currently available archaeological data.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 935, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 883})
