d the culmination of the process of Christianization begun under Constantine I at the beginning of the fourth century. General surveys of the Later Roman Empire or Late Antiquity often designate this constitution as the point when ‘Orthodox’ Christianity was made the official religion of the empire, following a brief rapprochement with paganism under Julian (r. 361-363). As such, it marked not only a dramatic shift from the religious syncretism which the Roman state had hitherto practiced, but was also to serve as a touchstone of Byzantine law. Henceforth, only Christians following the rite of the imperial church (those whom I will describe in what follows for the sake of convenience as ‘Orthodox’) could enjoy the rights of full Roman citizens. By contrast, over the following centuries various curtailments were introduced with regard to marriage, inheritance, officeholding and testamentary capacity for non-Orthodox or ‘heretical’ Christians, pagans, and Jews."'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1029, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 895})
