 much against Christianity, as in favour of toleration of the traditional cults: every people had their own customs and rituals, which were different paths to the truth. His memorandum was countered by two letters from Ambrose, bishop of Milan, to the emperor, which argued forcefully that it was the Christian duty of the emperor to fight for the church. After A. D. 382 with the partial exception of the (brief) reign of Eugenius (A.D. 392-394), the traditional cults did not receive the toleration Symmachus urged; and even Eugenius, himself a Christian, made only limited concessions to \'paganism\'. There was now only one true religio.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 957, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 879})
